---
export const title = 'Usage';
export const order = 100;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  <code>wc-kbd</code> shows a keyboard shortcut. Give it the keys joined by <code>+</code>;
  <code>mod</code> is Command on Apple platforms and Ctrl elsewhere, so one shortcut reads right
  for everyone.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <p style='display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.5rem; align-items: center;'>
    <wc-kbd keys='mod+k'></wc-kbd>
    <wc-kbd keys='mod+shift+p'></wc-kbd>
    <wc-kbd keys='alt+up'></wc-kbd>
    <wc-kbd keys='shift+enter'></wc-kbd>
  </p>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>Platform styles</h3>
<p>
  On Apple platforms modifiers show as symbols with no separators, as in macOS menus; elsewhere
  they show as names joined by <code>+</code>. The person's platform is detected;
  <code>platform="apple"</code> or <code>platform="other"</code> forces one, for documentation.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <div style='display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto auto; gap: 0.75rem 2rem; align-items: center;'>
    <span>Apple</span><wc-kbd keys='mod+alt+shift+backspace' platform='apple'></wc-kbd>
    <span>Windows, Linux</span><wc-kbd keys='mod+alt+shift+backspace' platform='other'></wc-kbd>
  </div>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>In text</h3>
<p>
  Without <code>keys</code>, the content shows as one key cap. The caps scale with the
  surrounding text.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <p>Press <wc-kbd>Esc</wc-kbd> to close, or <wc-kbd keys='mod+enter'></wc-kbd> to send.</p>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>Key names</h3>
<p>
  <code>mod</code>, <code>ctrl</code>, <code>meta</code> (<code>cmd</code>), <code>alt</code>
  (<code>option</code>), <code>shift</code>, <code>enter</code>, <code>escape</code>
  (<code>esc</code>), <code>tab</code>, <code>backspace</code>, <code>delete</code>,
  <code>space</code>, <code>up</code>, <code>down</code>, <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>,
  <code>pageup</code>, <code>pagedown</code>, <code>home</code>, <code>end</code> and
  <code>capslock</code>. Any other key shows as written, a single letter upper-cased;
  <code>ctrl++</code> is Ctrl and the plus key.
</p>

<h3>Accessibility</h3>
<p>
  Screen readers read symbols such as ⌘ and ⇧ badly or not at all. The key caps are hidden from
  them and a spelled-out copy, such as "Control+Shift+P", is read instead. The spoken and shown
  names are Peacock strings (<code>kbdControl</code>, <code>kbdCtrlShort</code>, ...) and can be
  translated with <code>setPeacockStrings()</code>.
</p>
